# [Create a chat completion]($heading.id('create-a-chat-completion'))

Send JSON to `POST /v1/chat/completions` with the ID returned by
[Models]($link.page('llmd/api/models').ref('read-the-model-id')).
This chat example uses `LiquidAI/LFM3-1.7B-FP8`.

```sh
curl -fsS http://127.0.0.1:8000/v1/chat/completions \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{
    "model": "LFM3-1.7B-FP8",
    "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "Reply with one short greeting."}],
    "max_completion_tokens": 64
  }'
```

## [Request fields]($heading.id('request-fields'))

| Request field | Type and default | Behavior |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `model` | String, required | ID from `GET /v1/models`. |
| `messages` | Array, required | Conversation history in order. A text message has a `role` and `content`. The loaded model's chat template determines supported roles and content types. |
| `max_completion_tokens` | Integer, optional | Limit generated tokens. The server also enforces its context limit. The older `max_tokens` field is used when `max_completion_tokens` is absent. |
| `stream` | Boolean, `false` | Set to `true` for server-sent events. |
| `stream_options.include_usage` | Boolean, `false` | Emit an additional usage event when streaming. |
| `temperature` | Number, optional | Request-level sampling temperature. |
| `top_p` | Number, optional | Request-level nucleus sampling threshold. Use `0` through `1`. |
| `tools` | Array, optional | Offer function definitions to a tool-capable model and chat template. |

For the request-level sampling behavior documented in
[LLMD 20261112.0](https://zml.ai/posts/llmd-20261112.0/#sampling-controls),
`temperature` and `top_p` override the CLI value, then the model recommendation
and fallback apply when needed. See [Sampling and decoding]($link.page('llmd/cli/sampling-and-decoding'))
for server defaults. Additional request fields and modalities depend on the
release and model. Verify that optional fields have the intended effect on
your release because the server can ignore unknown JSON fields.

## [Read the response]($heading.id('read-the-response'))

The JSON response includes `model`, `choices`, and `usage`. Read
`choices[0].message.content` for the text and `choices[0].finish_reason` for
the reason generation ended. A typical text response includes the following
fields. The generated text and token counts vary.

```json
{
  "model": "LFM3-1.7B-FP8",
  "choices": [
    {
      "index": 0,
      "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Hello!"},
      "finish_reason": "stop"
    }
  ]
}
```

# [Stream a chat completion]($heading.id('stream-a-chat-completion'))

Set `stream` to `true`. With curl, `-N` displays events as they arrive.

```sh
curl -fsSN http://127.0.0.1:8000/v1/chat/completions \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{
    "model": "LFM3-1.7B-FP8",
    "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "Reply with one short greeting."}],
    "max_completion_tokens": 64,
    "stream": true,
    "stream_options": {"include_usage": true}
  }'
```

The response uses `text/event-stream`. Parse each `data:` event as JSON and
append text from `choices[0].delta.content` when present. A usage event has an
empty `choices` array. A completed stream ends with `data: [DONE]`. For a streaming
tool call, assemble the `delta.tool_calls` fragments by their tool-call index
before parsing the arguments.

# [Call tools from a client]($heading.id('call-tools-from-a-client'))

Use a checkpoint and chat template with
[tool calling support](https://zml.ai/llmd/). Include function definitions in
`tools`, and ask for the function in the conversation. For
example, add this field to a chat request where the user asks for the time in
Paris.

```json
{
  "tools": [
    {
      "type": "function",
      "function": {
        "name": "get_local_time",
        "description": "Return the local time in a city",
        "parameters": {
          "type": "object",
          "properties": {"city": {"type": "string"}},
          "required": ["city"]
        }
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

When the model requests a call, read `choices[0].message.tool_calls`. Each
function call carries an `id`, a `function.name`, and `function.arguments`
encoded as a JSON string. The client validates and executes the function.
For the next completion, include the assistant message with its tool calls,
followed by a message with `role` set to `tool`, `tool_call_id` set to the
matching ID, and `content` set to the tool result.

# [Errors]($heading.id('errors'))

| Result | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `400 Bad Request` | Check required fields, JSON types, and the model's supported content types. |
| `400` with `error.code` set to `context_length_exceeded` | The tokenized prompt reached the server context cap. Shorten the conversation or review the server configuration. |
